#!/usr/bin/env -S uv run --script
# /// script
# requires-python = ">=3.11"
# dependencies = [
#     "capabilities-contract>=0.25,<1",
# ]
# ///
"""deployment - project deployment standard for agent-readable runtime specs.

Agent-invoked. Run `deployment help` to reload this contract before using the
tool. This capability is provider-neutral: it owns the local deployment shape
inside a consuming project, while provider capabilities such as `coolify` own
the external substrate. A project can use deployment without enabling Coolify.

For ContextKit-bound projects, generated Docker builds install every capability
in the catalogue, then initialize with the manager alone and never install or
call ContextKit: `capabilities init --codex --claude` (target-local bindings),
`capabilities doctor`, `capabilities hooks install --claude --codex` (session
hooks that run `capabilities session-start`), `capabilities build`. Generated
host bindings, compiled context, and machine-local bindings (.env.local) are
target-local build artifacts, not deployment inputs shipped from the repo.

USAGE
  deployment setup [--profile agent-box|agent-box-checkout|agent-box-shell]
                   [--target production]
                   [--provider coolify|dockerhost|manual]
                   [--with-telegram auto|yes|no] [--project-name NAME]
                   [--with-automations auto|yes|no]
                   [--force] [--dry-run]
      Full bootstrap for a non-technical deployment path. Creates the deployment
      declarations and the target's bundle in deployment/targets/<target>/:
      docker-compose.yaml, Dockerfile, Dockerfile.dockerignore, .env.example,
      entrypoint.sh, and (when needed) supervisord.conf. Agent-box setup
      embeds services in one supervised container, each under the node's
      service supervisor (`capabilities node supervise`): in files mode the descriptor-default services of
      capabilities the project enabled itself, with `enabled` available for
      separate Compose services, and the Telegram/automations flags are
      compatibility shorthands for per-capability service policy; in store
      mode (an agent whose records are in the store) every installed capability
      service, and those flags with any value but `auto` exit 6
      retired_option, since whether a service runs is the gate's answer on
      each node. If --dry-run is passed, print the files that would be written.

  deployment scaffold
      Alias for `deployment setup`.

  deployment init [--profile generic|agent-box|agent-box-checkout|agent-box-shell
                             |host-agents]
                  [--target production]
                  [--provider coolify|dockerhost|manual] [--force]
      Create the standard local deployment files:
        deployment/runtime.json
        deployment/targets/<target>/target.json
      Existing files are left untouched unless --force is passed. This command
      requires an existing project `capabilities/` envelope; it never writes
      `capabilities/settings.json`.

      The profile picks the substrate `deployment sync` compiles for.
      `agent-box` builds one supervised container carrying the project.
      `agent-box-checkout` builds the same container without the project: the
      image holds tools and the boot path, and the checkout arrives from Git on
      a volume the entrypoint clones into. The profile then keeps that body
      level with the branch it tracks, in both directions, so what was pushed
      reaches the box and what the agent commits there both survives a redeploy
      and reaches the remote. `agent-box-shell` builds the same container
      with no body at all, for an agent that lives wholly in the store: no
      project copy, no clone, no body sync, no ContextKit. Each boot binds an
      empty project root to the agent with `capabilities init --id
      $CAPABILITIES_PROJECT_ID`, wires the agent CLIs with `capabilities hooks
      install`, composes its context from the store with `capabilities build`,
      and starts the compiled services under their runners, where the gate
      for this node places them. `host-agents` supervises the same declared
      services as processes on the machine itself, on macOS: `sync` writes one
      launchd agent per service under `compiler.host.agents_dir`, at
      ProcessType Standard so the work a service starts gets the whole machine
      (its watchdog runs at Background), and `deployment next` hands over the
      launchctl steps. `generic` stays
      declaration-only. A Linux host uses a container profile, or `generic`
      with a supervisor the project owns. Run `deployment guide profiles` for
      the trade-offs.

  deployment sync [--check|--adopt]
      Compile the project's deployable-service descriptors into the runtime
      service graph and, for a container profile, one bundle per declared
      target (Compose, Dockerfile, ignore file, env example, entrypoint,
      supervisor configuration) in its folder. Every compiled service runs
      under the node's service supervisor, `capabilities node supervise` - a
      host agent through `deployment runner`, which hands it over. Which
      services are compiled
      depends on where the project's gate is kept:
      Files mode (no node in the gate): as before, only capabilities
      explicitly enabled in project settings are candidates, one enabled only
      at global scope contributes no service, and service_policy decides
      which run and how: auto_include, default_mode (enabled|embedded),
      capabilities.<name> auto|enabled|disabled|embedded and the descriptor's
      default_policy; service_policy.placement and services.<name>.placement
      (node|local|both) choose the substrate.
      Store mode (an agent whose records are in the store): every deployable
      descriptor of the installed catalogue, less capabilities.exclude, so
      the gate row naming each node, not the compile, decides whether a
      service runs there, and turning it on or off needs no recompile or
      redeploy. service_policy keeps only the shape: `shape`
      (embedded|container) for every service and `capabilities.<name>` for
      one; embedded runs under the agent container's Supervisor, whose one
      program for them is the node's service manager (`capabilities node
      run`), which keeps a program per service placed on the node and none
      for any other; container is a Compose service of its own. A service
      that declares only a machine form (service.machine, no service.deploy)
      has no container of its own, so a box carries it embedded whatever the
      shape, on that program's --services list. A store-mode
      runtime still carrying the retired switches (auto_include, default_mode,
      capabilities.<name> auto|enabled|disabled) or placement is reported as a
      warning naming each one, and sync rewrites it, keeping the shape it
      implied; `retired` in the result lists what it rewrote.
      A target's `node` is the id of the node its server runs as in the
      gate. The node resolves an id itself, from the
      ~/.config/capabilities/node-id that enrol writes (`capabilities node
      id --set`), so its bundle hands it none and `deploy` empties
      AGENT_NODE_ID on the application. For a node registered before ids,
      `node` is the name it is still keyed by: its bundle's Compose file
      sets AGENT_NODE_ID to it in every container, so every container of
      the box and every redeploy is that one node, and `deploy` sends it to
      the application. A target without one takes AGENT_NODE_ID from the
      server's environment, else its AGENT_NODE_NAME, which named the node
      before ids. The node's
      label is its row's (`capabilities node set <id> --label`). A malformed
      node (node_invalid) or a service environment_defaults also setting
      AGENT_NODE_ID beside it (node_declared_twice) stops the sync.
      The project's id (CAPABILITIES_PROJECT_ID, as the project resolves it
      at sync) is set with CAPABILITIES_PROJECT_ID_ROOT, the box's project
      root, in every container and, for an image that runs `capabilities
      init` in its build, in the image before that step.
      For host-agents, a loaded agent of a compiled service whose process
      is not `deployment runner <service>` or the `capabilities node supervise
      <service>` it hands over to (or, with no process, whose program
      is not the compiled launcher) runs its service outside the gate and the
      lease, as an agent loaded from an older compilation does: once the
      artifacts are written, sync boots it out, links the compiled agent in
      place of its installed file and bootstraps it, and lists each in
      `agents` (`replaced`, or `unloaded` or `failed` with the step, which is
      also an error). An agent never loaded stays a person's act (`next`).
      --check is read-only and exits 7 with structured drift details, and
      reports such an agent as an error instead of replacing it.
      --adopt explicitly replaces unmarked files at the runtime-declared
      managed artifact paths; external artifacts and overlays are never written.

  deployment runner <name> [--every SECONDS] [--restart POLICY] -- <command...>
      Run one capability service as the gate on this node says, until
      stopped; this is what a host launchd agent runs, and what every
      compiled supervisor entry ran before the node's service manager. It
      checks its arguments, this capability's gate and the read-only switch,
      then hands the service, in the same process, to the manager's
      supervisor: `capabilities node supervise <name> --every SECONDS
      --restart POLICY -- <command...>`, with SECONDS
      service_policy.reconcile_seconds when omitted (default 60, 5-3600) and
      POLICY unless-stopped. What the supervisor does - the gate and the lease
      on each reconcile, the restart policy, the hold on exit 4, the failure
      written onto the lease, the node reported seen, this node's row in
      capabilities_service_instances, the record it keeps under
      capabilities/deployment/state/runners/ - is the manager's (`capabilities
      help`, node supervise). It hands over where the manager answers `node
      supervise --probe`; with an older manager, or none found here, it
      supervises the service itself in the same way.

  deployment services
      This node - its id (node) and the label it gives itself (label) - and
      its view of each compiled service: what the gate answers for
      it here (placed, the row that answered: scope, node, enabled, service,
      and why),
      whether its runner is running it, a refusal it is held idle for, and
      who holds its lease (state, holder, expiry, and last_error: the exit
      code, stderr tail and time the service last failed with on the holder,
      else null). Writes nothing.

  deployment watchdog [--dry-run]
      One pass over the launchd agents this project supervises: ask each
      service's declared doctor whether it still works, and restart the ones
      that answer no. A supervisor restarts what died; this is for what is
      alive and doing nothing, which it cannot see. Two consecutive bad
      answers are required before a restart, and a cooldown keeps a service
      that a restart does not fix from being restarted every interval. A
      service whose doctor fails because the store does not answer
      (store_unreachable) is never restarted for it, since a restart cannot
      bring the store back and every restart opens its connections again: the
      watchdog waits, asking that doctor again after a pause that starts at the
      interval and doubles each time the store is still away, up to 30
      minutes, and the service reconnects by itself once the store returns.
      `deployment sync` installs this as its own launchd agent on the
      host-agents profile; run it by hand with --dry-run to see what it would
      do. Settings live under `compiler.host.watchdog` in runtime.json.

  deployment handover [--dry-run]
      Hand this project's host agents over to the node's service manager on
      a Mac (`capabilities node install`, which runs this in every folder
      whose services it will run): boot out each loaded launchd agent the
      host-agents compilation left in compiler.host.agents_dir - every
      service agent and the project watchdog, not the node-sync agents - and
      every other agent in ~/Library/LaunchAgents or that folder whose
      program or working directory lies inside the project, whatever
      compiled it, where its label ends in `watchdog` or in the name of an
      installed capability that bundles a service; and take its entry out
      of ~/Library/LaunchAgents, so a login does not load it again (a link
      is removed; a file put there by hand is kept beside the agents as
      <label>.plist.handed-over). Such an agent that runs neither is
      reported as kind other and left running, and the node manager's own
      agents (capabilities.*) are never touched. The compiled files stay.
      Run again it changes nothing. --dry-run changes nothing and answers
      the same. Answers {project, profile, dry_run, ok, agents [{label, kind
      (service|watchdog|other), service?, loaded, installed, action
      (handed_over, would_hand_over, kept, absent or failed, with step and
      detail), kept_at?}]}, or skipped where the project compiles no host
      agents and no agent of it is found; exit 6 when an agent could not be
      handed over.

  deployment machine sync|next [--text|--json]|status|watchdog [--dry-run]
  deployment machine handover [<name> ...] [--dry-run]
      The machine scope, on macOS: supervision for the services that run once
      per machine rather than once per project. `sync` asks the capabilities
      manager for the installed set and reads each capability's manifest
      snapshot; every one that declares `service.machine`, is allowed on this
      machine, and is not disabled in the machine declaration gets a launcher
      `capabilities-machine-<name>` and an agent
      `capabilities.machine.<name>.plist` running its `service.machine.command`
      in its expanded `service.machine.state` (home when it declares none), with
      the compiling shell's PATH, RunAtLoad, KeepAlive on unsuccessful exit,
      AbandonProcessGroup, so launchd never reaps the work the service started,
      and ProcessType Standard, so that work runs at standard priority on every
      core. It also writes `capabilities.machine.watchdog.plist`, at ProcessType
      Background, which runs
      `deployment machine watchdog`: one pass asking each machine service's
      `service.machine.doctor`, with the project watchdog's failure count,
      timeout, cooldown and wait for a store that does not answer, and `launchctl kickstart -k` after repeated
      failures. A loaded machine agent with no pid is never kickstarted: it is
      reported as `outside_launchd` with the pid when its doctor says the
      service runs as a copy started outside launchd, as `stopped` when it
      exited cleanly (last exit code 0), since its KeepAlive restarts only a
      failure, and otherwise as `restarting`. Every other installed capability
      is listed as skipped with the reason. A machine service with no joined project is still compiled; it
      idles. The declaration is `$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/deployment/machine.json`,
      {"watchdog": {...}, "services": {"<name>": {"disabled": true}}}, and a
      missing file means defaults; agents and logs land in
      `$XDG_STATE_HOME/deployment/machine/launchd/`, the same from any working
      directory. `next` prints the `ln -sf` and `launchctl bootstrap` steps,
      since handing an agent to launchd stays a person's act, and a loaded agent
      takes a recompiled file only through those steps; `status` reports each
      compiled agent, whether it is linked and loaded, its compiled
      `process_type`, the `spawn_type` launchd runs it with, and
      `spawn_type_current`, false while launchd still runs an earlier
      compilation, and writes nothing. `handover` hands the named machine
      services' agents (every compiled one when none is named) over to the
      node's service manager on a Mac (`capabilities node install`, which runs
      it for the machine services the node manager will run there): it boots
      out each loaded `capabilities.machine.<name>` and takes its entry out of
      ~/Library/LaunchAgents, as `deployment handover` does a project's, and
      the machine watchdog with them once no other machine service agent stays
      loaded. The compiled files stay. Run again it changes nothing; --dry-run
      changes nothing and answers the same. Answers {dry_run, ok, agents_dir,
      agents [{label, kind (service|watchdog), service?, loaded, installed,
      action, kept_at?}]}; exit 6 when an agent could not be handed over. Run
      `deployment guide profiles` for the model.

  deployment deploy [--target <name>]
      Check compiler drift, sync the body through node-<target> over SSH,
      provision a Coolify application in the project's existing environment
      (target.environment, default production; none is created), named
      <project>-<target> after the project's ContextKit name or recorded
      slug, and set that name, its base directory and its Compose location
      (the target's bundle) on every deploy, set environment through bulk stdin
      (with the runtime's compiler.build_args, which Coolify builds with), deploy,
      follow the deployment until Coolify ends it (at most 3600 s), then wait for healthy.
      The database is never sent: the node takes AGENTKIT_DB_* from the
      server's environment, and a deploy leaves those values standing.
      On failure request the previous release; the message says
      rollback=healthy, rollback=failed (<code>) or rollback=no previous
      release. A check of a started deployment that cannot reach the store
      (store_unreachable, store_busy) is tried again, up to 4 times; one that
      still cannot is not a failed deploy: nothing is rolled back, the answer
      on stdout says "deployment running, verification failed" with the
      deployment and the `coolify wait` that checks it, and deploy exits 5
      deployment_unverified. Requires agent-box-checkout, a
      committed branch and a granted connection. A target whose body_source
      is GitHub ({"kind": "github", "repository": "<owner>/<repo>"}, with
      deploy_key) prepares no server repository and pushes nothing: it
      refuses (commit_not_on_remote) unless the commit is on the GitHub
      branch, points the application at that repository and branch, sets
      AGENT_REPO_URL to its HTTPS URL, and builds exactly that commit.
  deployment rollback [--target <name>] [--to <commit>]
      Set the build args that release's runtime declares, roll back through
      Coolify and wait. Without --to use the previous release; a deploy that
      fails rolls back the same way.
  deployment logs [--target <name>] [--build] [--follow]
      Read runtime logs or the recorded deployment build log; --follow polls.
  deployment node add <label> --server <uuid> --version <tag> [--memory 2g]
                      [--services <name,...>] [--project <uuid>]
                      [--environment production] [--connection <id>]
                      [--wait 600] [--events]
      The whole path from nothing to a raised node of the standard image on
      a server Coolify already runs, one step after another: project, the
      Coolify project - --project, else the one named after the agent's
      slug, created when there is none (exit 6 project_ambiguous when
      several carry that name); environment, --environment in it, created
      when missing (`coolify projects environment create`); create, what
      `node create` does with --version and --memory; container, until the
      node's container runs (exit 5 node_not_running after --wait seconds);
      then what `node enrol` does with --services and --wait, as the steps
      id, key_pair, encryption_key, secrets, place (one per service) and
      store, and raised, until the node reports its agent running (exit 5
      node_not_raised). With --events each step is said as a JSON line on
      stdout, step {step, state started}, then done with its check or
      failed with its error {code, message}, beside the step's own fields
      (project, label, node, service); the stream ends in done {result},
      enrol's answer with project, environment, created and resource, or
      error {exit, error}. Without --events the answer alone is printed.
      What a step finds done it keeps (created false), so the same command
      again resumes after a failure: a node already labelled <label> on
      this server through this connection is that node, and one on another
      server or connection is refused (exit 6 node_exists) before anything
      is written; so are an AGENT_ENCRYPTION_KEY that does not open the
      vault and a connection that does not allow writes. A fresh server is
      installed with Coolify first, as before; node add starts from one
      Coolify runs.
  deployment node create <node> --project <uuid> --server <uuid>
                         --environment <name> --version <tag>
                         [--memory 2g] [--connection <id>]
  deployment node create <node> --project <uuid> --server <uuid>
                         --environment <name> --repository <url>
                         --branch <branch> --dockerfile <path> [--context <dir>]
                         [--deploy-key <uuid> | --github-app <uuid>]
                         [--docker-socket] [--host-dir <path>]...
                         [--memory 2g] [--connection <id>]
      For an agent that lives in the store: create a Coolify resource
      running the standard image ghcr.io/agenium-dev/agent-node:<tag>
      through the coolify connection (the only one granted, or --connection),
      in that project's existing environment on that server. It is a Compose
      service of one container with the memory limit, a volume of its own
      for each state path of the image (~/.config, ~/.local/state,
      ~/.claude, ~/.codex) and for ~/work, where the agent keeps its
      checkouts and git worktrees, and only CAPABILITIES_PROJECT_ID and
      CAPABILITIES_PROJECT_ID_ROOT (/app); no repository, no build, and no
      node identity in its environment. Then mint the node's id, twelve
      lowercase hex characters, and register the node row under it with
      <node> as its label, the name it is shown by, and the connection,
      server and
      resource (`capabilities node add`); a row that cannot be written takes
      the resource away again. The node starts idle, knowing no store; the
      answer's node.node is its id, which enrol puts on its config volume.
      Exit 6 node_exists (a node of this agent already labelled so),
      not_store_mode, environment_missing (3).
      With --repository instead of --version, Coolify builds the node's
      image on that server from the Dockerfile at --dockerfile in the
      repository's --branch, with --context as the build context (the
      repository root by default; the Dockerfile lies inside it) - a
      Dockerfile FROM the standard image, with no CI and no registry. The
      repository is cloned with a Coolify deploy key or GitHub App
      (`coolify sources` lists their uuids), else as public. The node is
      then a Coolify application with no domain, no automatic deploy on
      push and the same memory limit, keys and volumes, and only it
      takes host paths, each bound at the same path in the container:
      --docker-socket the host's /var/run/docker.sock, and each --host-dir
      an existing directory on the server (not /home/agent, /app or above
      them). Coolify never creates, writes or deletes a host path. The
      answer names the application and the build's deployment; `coolify
      wait <uuid> --deployment <uuid>` waits for the build before enrol.
      A step that fails once the application exists takes it away again.
  deployment node enrol <node> [--services <name,...>] [--wait 300]
      <node> is the node's id, or the label exactly one of the agent's
      nodes carries (node_ambiguous otherwise); so for exec and remove.
      Over SSH to the node's server and docker exec into its container:
      write the node's id onto its config volume with the node's own
      `capabilities node id --set <id>`, which the node resolves its id
      from from then on; make the node's X25519 key pair there (the private key stays in
      ~/.config/agent-node/node-key.pem, mode 0600) and record its public
      key on the node row; write the agent's encryption key,
      AGENT_ENCRYPTION_KEY as it resolves here, into the node's
      ~/.config/capabilities/shared.env on its config volume, which opens the
      agent's vault there (`capabilities secrets`; refused as key_mismatch
      when the key here is not the one the vault is kept under; the answer's
      key says whether it was placed and secrets_in_vault names, per
      capability, each secret key the node reads from the vault instead of
      a file); write the agent's other secrets - every secret key of a
      connection each capability the agent can use resolves here (enabled
      for the project or globally), read where each capability's
      `connections` says it is, except a name the vault holds and the
      coolify connection the node was created through, whose token the node
      does not hold - into each capability's credentials.env on the node's
      config volume. The answer's
      secrets_skipped names each secret key it did not carry and why (not
      set here, a value spanning lines, a key declared not secret, the
      withheld coolify connection, a capability whose `connections` gave no
      report), and environment_only names, per capability, each
      secret-looking key its service reads from its environment
      (service.deploy.environment, the database's aside) that no connection
      reports: one it reads from the process environment alone - a target's
      container environment, say - which no node receives until it is kept
      in a connection. Keys only, never values. Place each named service
      on the node (`capabilities place <name> --node <node>`), which takes an
      exclusive service off the node it ran on before (unplaced) and leaves
      where the capability is usable as it is;
      and write the store setting this project resolves with the node's own
      `capabilities store set`, last, since it is what the waiting node
      raises the agent on. Secrets travel on standard input and are never
      printed. Waits up to --wait seconds for the node to report its agent
      running (exit 5 node_not_raised otherwise). A store setting naming a
      root certificate file exits 6 store_setting_not_portable.
      The logins are not handed over: a person makes them on the node, over
      SSH to its server and `docker exec -it <container>` into the container
      labelled com.docker.compose.project=<resource> and
      com.docker.compose.service=agent (a node built from a repository: the
      one container labelled with the project alone) - `claude auth login`, `codex login
      --device-auth`, and `gh auth login` for the GitHub CLI (agent-node
      0.0.2 and later), through which git also reaches github.com over
      https. Each is kept on the node's volumes and outlives a new image
      tag.
  deployment node update <node> --version <tag> [--memory <size>] [--wait 600]
      Move a node of the standard image to ghcr.io/agenium-dev/agent-node:<tag>
      through the coolify connection its row names: write its Coolify
      service the whole Compose document `node create` writes for it, with
      the new tag (`coolify service set-compose`, which reads no field the
      token needs read:sensitive for), and redeploy it (`coolify deploy`)
      with its volumes kept, so its id, key, store setting, secrets, logins
      and work stay; then wait up to --wait seconds for its container to run
      that image, report healthy (when the image has a health check) and its
      agent state to read running. The document's service name and volume
      names are node create's; its environment is this agent's id and
      CAPABILITIES_PROJECT_ID_ROOT; its memory limit is --memory, a size as
      node create takes it (exit 6 input otherwise), or without it the one
      the node's container runs with. Before writing, it reads the node's
      one agent container over SSH (`docker inspect`) and refuses, changing
      nothing,
      unless each volume the document mounts is the one mounted at that
      path (<resource>_<name>) and nothing else is mounted but the image's
      own anonymous volumes (exit 6 node_volumes_unconfirmed); a path the
      container mounts nothing at gains the document's volume, new and
      empty (volumes_added), as on a node created before node create
      declared it, and what the old container kept at that path outside a
      volume is gone with it; and unless
      the container has a memory limit and sets exactly those two keys
      besides the image's environment, Coolify's own keys and a retired
      AGENT_NODE_NAME other than the node's id or AGENT_NODE_ID equal to it,
      which the new document drops (exit 6 node_document_unconfirmed). A node
      running the tag with no volume to gain and no new memory limit is not
      written, and is redeployed only when its container is not running, so running the
      update again finishes one that left the node down; a node with no
      agent container at all is refused (node_document_unconfirmed) and
      brought back first with `coolify deploy <uuid>`. Answers {ok, node,
      label, resource, version, image, previous_image, redeployed, memory,
      env_dropped, volumes, volumes_added, container, running_image, health,
      state}. A node built from a
      repository runs no tag and is refused (exit 6
      node_built_from_repository; rebuild it with `coolify deploy <uuid>`),
      as is a resource not running the standard image for this agent (exit
      6 node_resource_not_own) and a coolify without `service set-compose`
      (exit 6 provider_not_ready); nothing is changed then. A node that is
      not back in time exits 5 node_not_raised after printing the answer.
  deployment node remove <node> --yes
      Delete the node's Coolify resource with its volumes and configuration,
      then its node row. Only the resource the row names is touched; one
      already gone leaves only the row to remove. A resource whose agent
      container does not run the standard image - for a node built from a
      repository, the image Coolify built for that application - for this
      agent (its CAPABILITIES_PROJECT_ID) is refused (exit 6
      node_resource_not_own) and nothing is deleted.
      The host paths a node built from a repository binds are not its
      volumes: the socket and the directories and what is in them stay.
  deployment node exec <node> -- <command> [<arg>...]
      Run one command in the node's container, over SSH to its server and
      `docker exec -i` into the container enrol reaches, with no terminal:
      standard input is forwarded, standard output and standard error come
      back byte for byte as the command writes them (a JSON event stream
      such as `telegram login --events` or `whatsapp pair --events` reaches
      this machine line by line), and the exit code is the command's own -
      255 is SSH's own, when the server cannot be reached.
  deployment node login claude|codex [--target <name>] [--print-command]
      SSH with a terminal into the application's agent container for subscription
      login. --print-command prepares the command without attempting login.
  deployment node login <node> claude|codex|gh --events
      Run the tool's own headless sign-in in the node's container (`claude
      auth login`, `codex login --device-auth`, `gh auth login --web` for
      github.com), and speak it as JSON lines on stdout:
      link {url}, the page to open; code {code}, the code to enter there
      (codex, gh); code_needed, where the tool asks for the code the page
      gives to be pasted back (claude), answered by one line on stdin;
      waiting, while the tool waits for the sign-in to finish; then done
      {result {ok, node, tool}} or error {exit, error {code, message}},
      message the reason: login_failed with the tool's last words and its
      exit code, input_closed (exit 6) when stdin closes while code_needed
      waits, node_unreachable (255). Each event carries node and tool; the
      tool's own output goes to stderr. SIGTERM, SIGINT or SIGHUP cancels
      it: the tool on the node is stopped and the stream ends in error
      cancelled (exit 6). Each node is signed in on its own.
  deployment node login <node> <capability> [--connection <id>]
                        [--method <id>] --events
      Sign a device-bound connection (`connections.device_bound`, as
      telegram's and whatsapp's are) in on the node as a new device: the
      capability's own link ceremony from its manifest on the node (the
      first, or --method's id), run there with --connection as through
      `node exec`, its events passed on as it speaks them and stdin handed
      to it line by line. It never replaces a session: a node that already
      holds a live session for the connection is refused (exit 6
      already_signed_in) and --recreate is never passed. A connection that
      binds no expected_account_id while another of the agent's nodes holds
      a session for it is refused (exit 6 connection_unbound, naming the
      account to bind), since a first link that failed would take the
      shared connection out again. Before the ceremony one event devices
      {node, capability, connection, in_use [{node, label, account, live}],
      limit}: in_use the agent's other nodes the store reports holding a
      live session for it (`capabilities node state`), limit how many
      devices the service links to one account besides the phone (4 for
      whatsapp), else null. --connection may be left out where the
      capability has one connection. Cancel as above: the ceremony on the
      node gets SIGTERM, so a first link takes back what it made.
  deployment node sync [--target <name>]
      Fetch node-<target>, rebase and push; abort conflicts and report diverged.
      Optional target.mirror receives the synced branch. Host-agents compiles
      an interval job (target.sync_interval, default 60 seconds). A target
      whose body_source is GitHub answers state nothing_to_carry, touches no
      remote and gets no interval job: its node syncs with GitHub itself.
  deployment node list
      The nodes of this project's agent (`capabilities node list`), each
      with seen_seconds_ago and live. A node is live when its last_seen_at
      is within three reconciles (3 x service_policy.reconcile_seconds of
      deployment/runtime.json, default 60) of now: this is the definition of
      a node being live, and it holds for a Mac and for a node of the
      standard image alike, since the runner on either reports it each
      reconcile. A node never seen is not live. Whether a given service runs
      is not this: an exclusive service's live lease is the proof it runs,
      and on which node (`deployment services`). Answers {ok, project,
      agent_id, reconcile_seconds, live_within_seconds, nodes [{...,
      seen_seconds_ago, live}]}; the manager's refusals pass through.
  deployment node status [--target <name>] [--json]
      JSON host, release, Git sync, harness, store and per-service state.
      Git sync is against the node remote, or the GitHub branch for a target
      whose body_source is GitHub.
      store is the node's database in force, resolved in its container through
      the shared library: level, sources and setting redacted, reachable,
      tls_enforced.
      Each service carries `node` (its Supervisor or container state there),
      `local` (its launchd agent here), `placement` in files mode, and, from
      `deployment services` run in the node's container, `gate`, `running`,
      `lease` and any `refusal`; node_id is the id the node answers to in
      the gate and node_name the label it gives itself (its
      AGENT_NODE_NAME). A target that declares `node` adds
      declared_node_name, and an error node_name_mismatch while the node
      answers to another id.
      leftovers is what no service accounts for, read in the container:
      processes that are neither a Supervisor program nor descended from one
      (pid, ppid, age_seconds, command), development sessions open longer than
      a day or damaged (id, capability, age_hours, damaged), and the size of
      /tmp and of the uv cache in bytes.

  deployment doctor [--target <name>]
      Validate deployment/runtime.json, deployment/targets/*.json, and the deployment
      references. Reports provider readiness as local facts only: whether the
      provider capability is installed/enabled here. It does not call Coolify,
      SSH, Docker, or any remote provider. For host-agents it reports each
      compiled agent as launchd holds it, and a loaded one running its
      service outside `deployment runner` as an error naming its label. On a
      Mac whose node manager runs the project's services (records in the
      store, and capabilities.node, from `capabilities node install`, in
      place) the compiled service agents are left unloaded, as `deployment
      handover` leaves them, and only one loaded beside the node manager is
      reported, as a warning; `deployment next` there lists only the agents
      that carry the body to a node, and starts, stops and restarts the
      services through `capabilities service`.

  deployment runtime
      Print deployment/runtime.json.

  deployment targets [--target <name>]
      Print target declarations from deployment/targets/.

  deployment plan [--target <name>]
      Print an execution-neutral deployment plan. The plan names the provider
      adapter that would execute the target, plus command hints, but performs no
      remote operation.

  deployment next [--target <name>] [--text|--json]
      Human next steps after setup: what to review, what secrets to enter, how
      to create the provider resource, first-login commands, and validation.

Contract commands:
  deployment help
  deployment stub
  deployment manifest --json
  deployment guide [agent-node|bootstrap|profiles|standard|targets]
  deployment migrate [status]
  deployment connections
  deployment ids list|get <label>|set <label> <value>|rm <label> [--note <text>]
  deployment refs
  deployment inventory [--network]

FILES
  deployment/runtime.json
      The project's runtime declaration: profile, services, service-level
      required env, state volumes, runtime capability exclusions, and
      service_policy (see `deployment sync`; reconcile_seconds in either mode) and compiler layout. A container runtime
      that names no artifact path compiles one bundle per target (below).
      compiler.artifacts declares the
      Dockerfile, entrypoint, env example, dockerignore, and optional supervisor paths;
      compose_file declares the generated base Compose file; either one keeps
      the runtime on those declared paths instead of per-target bundles;
      compiler.compose_overlays declares untouched project-owned overlays;
      compiler.container supplies agent_home and project_root for portable mounts;
      compiler.container.persistent_manager true (container profiles) keeps the
      capabilities manager's whole home on named volumes, its launcher
      directory first on PATH: the node keeps the tools it installs, and each
      boot updates the manager to the newest release of the official
      source's tracked branch, never an older one than it has, and brings
      the catalogue capabilities to the image's catalogue;
      compiler.container.checkouts (agent-box-checkout) lists other
      repositories the box works on, each {path, url, branch, register}: each
      boot clones one whose path is absent or empty, never touches one already
      checked out, and with register "official" registers it as the manager's
      integration checkout unless the manager already names it;
      compiler.build_args is the only source of generated Compose build args.
      A service environment_defaults mapping overrides descriptor env defaults.

  Runtime-declared compiler artifact paths
      A string path is compiler-managed for compatibility. Object declarations
      use {path, ownership}, where ownership is managed or external. External
      files must exist but are excluded from drift, sync, and adoption. Setup
      declares none, so its runtime compiles per-target bundles; declared local
      Compose overlays are external.

  deployment/targets/<target>/
      One folder per deployment target. target.json chooses a provider
      (`coolify`, `dockerhost`, or `manual`) and carries only the adapter-facing
      resource handle needed to execute that target; a declaration still at
      deployment/targets/<target>.json is read until it is moved. Beside it,
      sync compiles the target's bundle: docker-compose.yaml (build context
      ../../.., the project root), Dockerfile, Dockerfile.dockerignore (Docker
      reads it for that Dockerfile), .env.example, entrypoint.sh,
      supervisord.conf when Supervisor runs, and body-sync.sh on the checkout
      profile. The project root gains only .gitattributes on that profile.

  Capabilities in a container image
      The image installs every capability in the official catalogue at
      CAPABILITIES_REF (default main) with `capabilities install <name>
      --allow`, a core one arriving enabled by the manager's first-install
      rule, and each boot brings the registry to that catalogue: it installs
      any catalogue capability the registry lacks, reinstalls from it one
      installed from another catalogue, updates one whose payload differs from
      it, and leaves one installed from elsewhere as it is, so a node whose
      manager home persists runs the catalogue its image names. Whether a
      capability is usable is its gate's and its connections' answer, not
      whether it is installed. The runtime's capabilities.exclude list keeps
      operator-only tools out of the image without changing their project
      policy on the host.

  capabilities/deployment/reference/*.md
      Optional project-specific operational models. Reference files are neither
      created nor required by deployment init or setup. When present, they hold
      genuine project-specific deployment context rather than pointers
      duplicating runtime.json or target JSON schemas. Loaded on demand by
      `deployment refs` and the manager's generated capability context.

EXIT CODES
  0 success
  3 not found (no project, no runtime, unknown target)
  4 project policy refused this capability
  5 the store cannot be reached (migrate)
  6 malformed input, validation failure, or init conflict
  7 deployment sync --check drift
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
